    "0 | The **two pointers** pattern walks an array with two indices at once, so one pass does more. | - | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.",
    "1 | Start one at each end and move them inwards. Reversing is the simplest example. | - | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.",
    "2 | Reverse in place: **left** starts at the first item, **right** at the last. | reverse-start | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6. left at index 0. right at index 5.",
    "3 | Swap **1** and **6**, then move both pointers one step in. | reverse-swap | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 6, 2, 3, 4, 5, 1. left at index 0. right at index 5.",
    "4 | Swap **2** and **5**, then move both pointers one step in. | reverse-swap | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 6, 5, 3, 4, 2, 1. left at index 1. right at index 4.",
    "5 | Swap **3** and **4**, then move both pointers one step in. | reverse-swap | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1. left at index 2. right at index 3.",
    "6 | **left** and **right** have met in the middle, so it’s reversed. Swaps made: 3. | reverse-done | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.",
    "7 | Each swap moved both pointers in: n ÷ 2 swaps, **O(n)** time, and no second array, **O(1)** space. | - | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1. | ⏸ chapter-end",
    "8 | Which two items add up to **10**? Trying every pair is **O(n²)**. A sorted array gives a shortcut. | - | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11.",
    "9 | Find two items that add up to **10**. **left** starts at the smallest, **right** at the biggest. | pair-start | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. left at index 0. right at index 5.",
    "10 | 1 + 11 = **12**, more than 10. For a smaller sum, **right** moves left. | pair-right | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. left at index 0. right at index 4. Sum 12.",
    "11 | 1 + 8 = **9**, less than 10. For a bigger sum, **left** moves right. | pair-left | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. left at index 1. right at index 4. Sum 9.",
    "12 | 3 + 8 = **11**, more than 10. For a smaller sum, **right** moves left. | pair-right | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. left at index 1. right at index 3. Sum 11.",
    "13 | 3 + 6 = **9**, less than 10. For a bigger sum, **left** moves right. | pair-left | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. left at index 2. right at index 3. Sum 9.",
    "14 | 4 + 6 = **10**. Found the pair, at index 2 and 3. | pair-found | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. left at index 2. right at index 3. Found 4 at index 2. Sum 10.",
    "15 | Each step ruled one item out for good, so it takes at most n − 1 checks: **O(n)**. | - | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11.",
    "16 | It works because the array is **sorted**: moving left can only raise the sum, moving right only lowers it. | - | Array of 6 items, 6 slots: 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11. | ⏸ chapter-end",
    "17 | Pointers can also move at different speeds. **read** checks every item; **write** moves only to keep one. | - | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5.",
    "18 | Remove repeats in place. **1** is kept; **write** marks where the next new value goes. | dedupe-start | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 1. write at index 1.",
    "19 | **1** is the same as the last value kept: skip it. | dedupe-skip | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 1. write at index 1.",
