    "21 | **3** is new: copy it down to index 2, and **write** moves on. | dedupe-keep | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 3. write at index 3.",
    "22 | **3** is the same as the last value kept: skip it. | dedupe-skip | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 4. write at index 3.",
    "23 | **3** is the same as the last value kept: skip it. | dedupe-skip | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 5. write at index 3.",
    "24 | **4** is new: copy it down to index 3, and **write** moves on. | dedupe-keep | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 3, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 6. write at index 4.",
    "25 | **5** is new: copy it down to index 4, and **write** moves on. | dedupe-keep | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 7. write at index 5.",
    "26 | **5** is the same as the last value kept: skip it. | dedupe-skip | Array of 9 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 3, 4, 5, 5. read at index 8. write at index 5.",
    "27 | The first 5 items are the unique values; the other 4 are dropped. One pass, no new array. | dedupe-done | Array of 5 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.",
    "28 | The unique values are packed at the front in one pass, with no second array: **O(n)** time, **O(1)** space. | - | Array of 5 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. | ⏸ chapter-end",
    "29 | Reach for two pointers when the data is **in order**, or the work goes from both ends inwards. | - | Array of 5 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.",
    "30 | Classic uses: pair sums, reversing, removing repeats, merging sorted lists, checking palindromes. | - | Array of 5 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.",
    "31 | If the brute-force answer checks every pair, order plus two pointers often cuts O(n²) to **O(n)**. | - | Array of 5 items, 9 slots: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.",
